The '''spotlight effect''' is the [[Psychology|psychological]] phenomenon by which people tend to believe they are being noticed more than they really are.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Example |year=2000 |title=A cited paper}}</ref> The term was used in a study published in 2000.<ref name="a" />

People tend to overestimate how much others notice their appearance and behaviour.{{sfn|Example|2000|p=2}} The effect is described as a form of [[egocentric bias]].

== Research ==
In one experiment, students wore a shirt they found embarrassing and estimated how many peers noticed it. The students who wore the shirt guessed that about half of the room noticed, while the real share was lower, close to 25% of the peers in the room.

